# Package structure

The generated backend uses a straightforward Spring Boot package structure:

```text
{{packagePath project.packageName}}/
  api/generated/ Contract-first Spring API interfaces and models (when used)
  config/       Shared Spring configuration
  controller/   REST controllers
  domain/       Domain models and JPA entities
  exception/    Application exceptions and HTTP status mappings
  repository/   Spring Data repositories
  security/     Authentication and authorization configuration
  service/      Use cases and application services
  web/
    dto/        Request and response types
    mapper/     Manual HTTP and domain mapping
```

## Request flow

1. A controller validates an HTTP request DTO.
2. A mapper converts the DTO to a domain entity.
3. A service applies use-case behavior and calls a repository.
4. The controller returns a mapped response DTO.

## Conventions

- Use constructor injection for Spring-managed classes.
- Keep HTTP request and response types in `web.dto`.
- Keep DTO conversions in `web.mapper`.
- Put JPA annotations on entities in `domain` and Spring Data interfaces in `repository`.
- Put schema changes in {{#if (eq project.options.migrationTool "liquibase")}}Liquibase changelogs under `src/main/resources/db/changelog/changes`{{else}}Flyway migrations under `src/main/resources/db/migration`{{/if}}.
- Add application-specific code inside the protected regions in generated files.

